"""Chapters 1 to 3. Items: ('h2'|'h3'|'h5', text), ('p', text), ('bullets', [..]), ('table', header, rows, caption), ('fig', file, caption, width_in)."""
from data import COLD, DS, HW, OPS, TESTS, exp, f, lat, loc, startup, thr

TITLE = "Online Library Management System using Microservices Architecture"
n_services = 8
total_loc = sum(v for k, v in loc.items() if k != "frontend")

CH1 = [
    ("h2", "CHAPTER 1:  INTRODUCTION"),
    ("p", "A library looks like a simple organisation: it owns books, it has members, members borrow books and bring them back, and a member who is late pays a fine. Library software has traditionally been written as one program with one database. That is easy to start with, but as the number of users and features grows, every change touches the same code and the same tables, the whole program has to be restarted for a small fix, and the part that is under the heaviest load (for example catalogue search) cannot be scaled without scaling everything else."),
    ("p", "Microservices architecture answers this by splitting the system into small services that are built, deployed and scaled independently. Each service owns one business capability and its own data, and services talk to each other only through well-defined interfaces. The price of this freedom is a new set of problems: how clients find the services, how a request that crosses several services is kept consistent when one of them is down, how the system is secured, and how a developer can see what is happening inside it."),
    ("p", f"This project builds an Online Library Management System (OLMS) as a complete microservices system for the course IS373TA Microservices Architecture. It consists of {n_services} Java services (a configuration server, a service registry, an API gateway and five business services), a web front end, a PostgreSQL server with one database per business service, a RabbitMQ message broker and a monitoring stack of Zipkin, Prometheus and Grafana, all started with one command. The catalogue is filled with {DS['n']:,} real books from the public Goodbooks-10k dataset, and every number reported in this document was measured on the running system."),
    ("p", "The project does not only show that the services can be drawn on paper. It answers four questions that every real microservices system has to answer: where does each service keep its data, who is allowed to call what, what happens to a half-finished operation when a neighbour fails, and how can a developer follow one request across services. The report documents the design decisions, the code, the tests and the measurements that answer them."),
    ("h3", "1.1  Terminology"),
    ("p", "The following terms are used throughout the report. They are defined once here so that later chapters can use them without repeating the explanation."),
    ("table", ["Term", "Meaning in this project"], [
        ["Microservice", "A small, independently deployable program that owns one business capability and its data (book, member, lending, fine, notification)."],
        ["API gateway", "The single entry point for clients. It routes requests to services, checks the login token and enforces role rules."],
        ["Service registry", "Eureka server in which every service registers itself, so that other services find it by name instead of by address."],
        ["Config server", "Spring Cloud Config server that hands shared settings (loan period, fine per day, tracing, metrics) to every service."],
        ["Database per service", "Each service has its own PostgreSQL database and its own login; no service reads another service's tables."],
        ["Circuit breaker", "A guard around a remote call that stops calling a failing service for a while, so the caller fails fast instead of hanging."],
        ["Saga", "A way to finish an operation that spans several services as a sequence of local transactions with compensating steps, instead of one distributed transaction."],
        ["Transactional outbox", "An event is written to a table in the same database transaction as the business change, and a relay later sends it to the broker. No change is announced twice or lost."],
        ["Idempotency key", "A unique key sent with a request so that repeating the request does not repeat its effect (used for stock changes)."],
        ["JWT", "JSON Web Token: a signed token issued at login that carries the user's role and is checked by the gateway on every request."],
        ["Trace", "The record of one request as it travels through several services, shown by Zipkin as a tree of timed spans."],
        ["Bounded context", "A part of the domain with its own vocabulary and rules. Here: Catalogue, Membership, Lending, Fines, Notifications."],
    ], "Terminology"),
    ("h3", "1.2  Scope and Relevance"),
    ("p", "The scope of the project is the complete life cycle of a small but realistic system: domain analysis with the SEED(S) process, design of service boundaries, implementation, containerisation, security, observability, testing and measurement. The user-visible functions are catalogue management and search, member management, issuing and returning books, automatic fines for late returns, notifications and a loan history. Two roles exist: the librarian, who may manage everything, and the member, who may browse the catalogue and see only their own loans, fines and notifications."),
    ("p", "The topic is relevant because the same problems appear in every domain that is built from services: online shops, banks and hospitals all need consistent multi-step operations, login at the edge, resilience to partial failure and end-to-end visibility. The library domain is small enough to be understood in a minute, which leaves the effort for the architectural questions instead of the business rules."),
    ("p", "The study is deliberately bounded. Everything runs on one laptop under Docker Compose; the Kubernetes manifests are provided but were not applied to a cluster during this work. There is no payment, no real e-mail or SMS delivery (a notification is a stored message) and no production hardening such as certificate management or secret stores. These limits are listed again in Chapter 7."),
    ("h3", "1.3  Motivation"),
    ("p", "The course syllabus covers the SEED(S) design process, service boundaries, data ownership, event sourcing and CQRS, containers, Kubernetes and the management of change. Reading about these topics is not the same as seeing them work together. The motivation was to build one system in which each topic has a concrete place, and in which a student or examiner can start the system, click through it, break a service on purpose and watch the system react."),
    ("p", "A second motivation came from the first version of this project. It already worked, but it had three weaknesses that the syllabus itself points at: the services kept their data in memory, anyone could call any API, and the stock update in the book service and the loan record in the lending service were two separate writes, so a crash between them could leave the two services disagreeing. The final version removes each of these and measures the result."),
    ("h3", "1.4  Problem Statement"),
    ("p", "Design and implement a library management system as a set of microservices with their own databases, in which a librarian can issue and take back books and members can follow their own loans, such that multi-service operations stay consistent when a service or the message broker fails, access is controlled by role, and every request can be traced and measured."),
    ("h3", "1.5  Objectives"),
    ("bullets", [
        "O1. Decompose the library domain into services with the SEED(S) process, domain-driven design and event storming.",
        "O2. Implement five business services, an API gateway, a service registry and a configuration server with Spring Boot and Spring Cloud.",
        f"O3. Fill the catalogue with a real dataset ({DS['n']:,} books from Goodbooks-10k) instead of invented sample rows.",
        "O4. Build a web front end with a login page and separate views for librarians and members.",
        "O5. Secure the system with JWT login and role rules enforced at the gateway.",
        "O6. Keep data consistent without a distributed transaction, using a saga, a transactional outbox, idempotency keys and idempotent consumers.",
        "O7. Make the system observable with distributed traces (Zipkin), metrics (Prometheus) and dashboards (Grafana).",
        "O8. Package the system with Docker Compose and Kubernetes manifests, test it, measure it and report the results, including the defects found, honestly.",
    ]),
    ("h3", "1.6  Report Organisation"),
    ("p", "Chapter 2 reviews the literature and positions the work. Chapter 3 lists the functional, non-functional, hardware and software requirements. Chapter 4 explains the underlying concepts, describes the dataset and presents the design: the SEED(S) artefacts, the architecture, the data model and the saga. Chapter 5 describes the implementation with code excerpts and the testing, including the defects that testing found. Chapter 6 presents the measurements, the fault-injection experiments and live screenshots of the running system. Chapter 7 concludes with limitations and future scope. The appendices contain a dataset sample and the list of API operations."),
    ("h3", "1.7  Summary"),
    ("p", "This chapter introduced the problem of building a library system from independent services, stated the objectives and fixed the scope. The remaining chapters document how each objective was met and how well the result performs."),
]

CH2 = [
    ("h2", "CHAPTER 2:  LITERATURE SURVEY"),
    ("p", "This chapter reviews the books, papers and specifications that the design relies on. They are grouped by the problem they address. The list is not a survey of the whole field; it contains the works that directly shaped a decision in this project, and each subsection ends by saying which decision that was."),
    ("h3", "2.1  Literature Review"),
    ("h5", "2.1.1  Foundations of microservices"),
    ("p", "Lewis and Fowler [5] describe microservices as small services, each running in its own process and communicating through lightweight mechanisms, organised around business capabilities and deployed independently. Dragoni et al. [6] trace how the style grew out of service-oriented architecture and discuss its benefits and open problems. Newman [2] and Nadareishvili [1], both prescribed in the syllabus, give the practical rules of thumb: services should be small enough to be owned by one team, loosely coupled and highly cohesive. Carnell [4] shows the same ideas with Spring Boot and Spring Cloud, which is the technology used here. Percival and Gregory [3] explain architecture patterns, including events and message buses, that are independent of the language. The programming interfaces of the services follow the REST style described by Fielding [16]."),
    ("h5", "2.1.2  Finding service boundaries"),
    ("p", "Evans [7] introduced domain-driven design and the bounded context: a part of the domain inside which every term has one meaning. Richardson [8] uses bounded contexts to decide where one service ends and the next begins. The syllabus adds event storming and a sizing formula as practical tools. In this project the contexts Catalogue, Membership, Lending, Fines and Notifications were found by event storming and checked against the rule that a service should have one reason to change."),
    ("h5", "2.1.3  Data ownership, consistency and events"),
    ("p", "Garcia-Molina and Salem [9] proposed sagas for long-lived transactions: a sequence of local transactions, each with a compensating action that undoes it. Richardson [8] applies the idea to microservices and also describes the transactional outbox, in which an event is stored in the same transaction as the state change and published afterwards. Kleppmann [12] explains why distributed transactions are avoided and how idempotence makes retries safe. Hohpe and Woolf [11] catalogue messaging patterns, including the idempotent receiver. These four sources are the basis of the saga, the outbox, the idempotency keys and the duplicate-safe notification consumer in Chapter 5."),
    ("h5", "2.1.4  Resilience"),
    ("p", "Nygard [10] describes the circuit breaker, timeouts and bulkheads as ways to stop one failing dependency from taking down its callers. The Resilience4j library used here implements the circuit breaker. One practical lesson, confirmed by the measurements in Chapter 6, is that a circuit breaker must be told which failures to count: a business rejection such as \"no copies available\" is not a sign that the book service is unhealthy."),
    ("h5", "2.1.5  Security at the edge"),
    ("p", "The JSON Web Token standard [17] defines a compact signed token that carries claims. A gateway that verifies the token and applies role rules protects every service behind it with one piece of code. This is the approach taken here; it is also the reason the gateway must be the only entry point, a point revisited in the limitations."),
    ("h5", "2.1.6  Observability"),
    ("p", "Sigelman et al. [18] describe Dapper, the Google system that popularised distributed tracing: a request carries an identifier through all services, and each service records timed spans. Zipkin, used here, follows the same model. Metrics are collected by Prometheus and shown in Grafana; the three pillars (logs, metrics, traces) are tied together by putting the trace identifier into every log line."),
    ("h5", "2.1.7  Organisation and deployment"),
    ("p", "Conway [14] observed that systems mirror the communication structure of the organisation that builds them. Skelton and Pais [13] turn this into team topologies: stream-aligned teams that own a service end to end, supported by a platform team. The syllabus uses this as the basis of the operating model, discussed in Section 4.3. For deployment, Burns et al. [15] describe the lessons from Borg and Omega that led to Kubernetes, and the Docker and Kubernetes documentation define the objects used in the manifests."),
    ("h3", "2.2  Summary of the Reviewed Works"),
    ("table", ["#", "Work", "Topic", "Used in this project for"], [
        ["[1]", "Nadareishvili, Microservices: Up and Running (2020)", "Building microservices step by step", "Overall process, SEED(S) vocabulary"],
        ["[2]", "Newman, Building Microservices, 2nd ed. (2021)", "Design, data, deployment, change", "Service size, database per service, migration advice"],
        ["[3]", "Percival and Gregory, Architecture Patterns with Python (2020)", "Events, message bus, CQRS", "Event-driven interaction between services"],
        ["[4]", "Carnell, Spring Microservices in Action (2017)", "Spring Cloud stack", "Config server, Eureka, gateway, Feign, circuit breaker"],
        ["[5]", "Lewis and Fowler, Microservices (2014)", "Definition of the style", "Terminology and characteristics"],
        ["[6]", "Dragoni et al., Microservices: Yesterday, Today, and Tomorrow (2017)", "History and open problems", "Motivation, limitations"],
        ["[7]", "Evans, Domain-Driven Design (2003)", "Bounded contexts", "Service boundaries"],
        ["[8]", "Richardson, Microservices Patterns (2018)", "Saga, outbox, API gateway", "Saga and outbox design"],
        ["[9]", "Garcia-Molina and Salem, Sagas (1987)", "Long-lived transactions", "Compensation concept"],
        ["[10]", "Nygard, Release It!, 2nd ed. (2018)", "Stability patterns", "Circuit breaker, timeouts"],
        ["[11]", "Hohpe and Woolf, Enterprise Integration Patterns (2003)", "Messaging patterns", "Idempotent receiver, topic exchange"],
        ["[12]", "Kleppmann, Designing Data-Intensive Applications (2017)", "Distributed data", "Why no distributed transaction, retries"],
        ["[13][14]", "Skelton and Pais, Team Topologies; Conway (1968)", "Teams and architecture", "Operating model"],
        ["[15]", "Burns et al., Borg, Omega, and Kubernetes (2016)", "Container orchestration", "Kubernetes manifests"],
        ["[17]", "Jones, Bradley and Sakimura, RFC 7519 (2015)", "JSON Web Token", "Login and role claims"],
        ["[18]", "Sigelman et al., Dapper (2010)", "Distributed tracing", "Zipkin traces"],
        ["[19]", "Zajac, Goodbooks-10k (2017)", "Book dataset", "Catalogue data"],
    ], "Reviewed works and how each is used"),
    ("h3", "2.3  Research Gap and Positioning"),
    ("p", "The gap addressed is one of integration and evidence rather than of a new algorithm. Individual patterns are well documented, but tutorial-level examples frequently show them one at a time, with in-memory data, without login and without measurements. This project combines them in one running system and checks each claim by experiment."),
    ("table", ["Point that is often left open", "What this project does", "Evidence"], [
        ["Data of each service lives in memory or in one shared database", "One PostgreSQL database and one login per service", "Section 6.1: permission denied across databases"],
        ["Anyone can call every API", "JWT login and role rules at the gateway, internal endpoints blocked", "9 unit tests and live checks (401, 403, 200)"],
        ["Two writes in two services assumed to succeed together", "Saga with compensation, outbox, idempotency keys", "Experiments E1 to E7"],
        ["Circuit breaker enabled with default settings", "Breaker records only server errors and timeouts; timeouts shortened", "Defects D5 and D6, {fig:outage}"],
        ["Monitoring added at the end or not at all", "Traces, metrics and a provisioned dashboard from the start", "{fig:trace} to {fig:grafana}"],
        ["No numbers", "Latency, throughput, start-up time, memory, event delay measured and compared with the first version", "Section 6.2"],
    ], "Points often left open in tutorial systems, what this project does, and the evidence"),
    ("p", "The positioning is modest. None of the individual techniques is new, and the numbers come from one laptop; they show how the system behaves, not how far it scales."),
    ("h3", "2.4  Summary"),
    ("p", "The reviewed works give a clear recipe: bounded contexts for boundaries, a database per service, sagas and outbox for consistency, circuit breakers for resilience, tokens at the gateway for security and traces for visibility. The next chapter turns this recipe into requirements."),
]

